WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:06.780
A synthetic scenario on ChainLens's demo site: a withdrawal to a clean-looking address.

00:00:07.130 --> 00:00:11.008
ChainLens recommends holding it for a compliance officer.

00:00:11.008 --> 00:00:11.280
Why?

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:13.195
Rewind.

00:00:13.195 --> 00:00:18.990
A user had asked an exchange to send 500,000 USDT to that address.

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:23.027
A blacklist only knows addresses someone already reported.

00:00:23.027 --> 00:00:27.160
This one isn't listed, so a blacklist alone would let it through.

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:33.800
ChainLens follows the money backwards, upstream to a scam's collection wallet,

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:38.760
fed by victims, split across mule wallets, and merged back in.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:42.440
The evidence: the path the money took.

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:52.370
Two engines judge every request: a rulebook of known laundering patterns, and a model that reads the network's shape.

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:57.570
A person makes the final call, with the evidence and a draft report in hand.

00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:03.060
On the demo site, both engines point the same way: hold.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:08.185
A newer trick: money that rests for minutes, then moves on.

00:01:08.185 --> 00:01:10.380
The rules alone would release it.

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:16.378
The model spots the shape and asks for a closer look by a person: enhanced review.

00:01:16.378 --> 00:01:17.824
That's all it can do.

00:01:17.824 --> 00:01:21.750
It can't hold a withdrawal, or downgrade one, on its own.

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:25.640
Released by the rules, flagged by the model.

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:29.585
And it mustn't punish the innocent.

00:01:29.585 --> 00:01:32.880
An exchange paying thirty users looks like laundering,

00:01:33.230 --> 00:01:34.910
unless you know it's an exchange.

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:38.251
Labelled: released.

00:01:38.251 --> 00:01:43.660
The system's own what-if says that without the label, all thirty would be held.

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:47.383
Same withdrawal, two answers.

00:01:47.383 --> 00:01:53.970
ChainLens's screening model was trained and tested on synthetic scenarios, not real cases.
